response:
 By determinant form, we compute
\[\mathbf{a} \cdot (\mathbf{b} \times (\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b})) =
\frac{1}{2}
\begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{a} & \mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{b} \\
\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{b} & \mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{b}
\end{vmatrix}
\begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{b} \cdot \mathbf{b} & \mathbf{b} \cdot \mathbf{a} \\
\mathbf{b} \cdot \mathbf{a} & \mathbf{b} \cdot \mathbf{a}
\end{vmatrix}.
We also have the well known
\[ \|\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b}\|^2 = (\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b}) \cdot
(\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b}) =
(\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{a})(\mathbf{b} \cdot \mathbf{b})
- (\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{b})^2 = 2,\]
and because $\|\mathbf{a}\|^2 = (\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{a}) \ge 0$ and
also $\|\mathbf{b}\|^2 = (\mathbf{b} \cdot \mathbf{b}) \ge 0,$ we must conclude that
either (1) $ \mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{a} = 0,$ $\mathbf{b} \cdot \mathbf{b} = 2,$
$\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{b} = -2,$ or else that (2) $\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{a} =
2,$ $\mathbf{b} \cdot \mathbf{b} = 0,$ and $\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{b} = 2.$  Either
way, the given determinant is
\[ \frac{1}{2}
\begin{vmatrix}
0 & -2 \\
-2 & -2
\end{vmatrix}
\begin{vmatrix}
2 & 2 \\
2 & 2
\end{vmatrix}
= 0.
\end{eqnarray*} \]~and vice versa. Thus, \[\mathbf{a} \cdot (\mathbf{b} \times (\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b})) = \boxed{0}.~\] </answer>

